{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 13, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during a turbulent era marked by rising Muslim power under Saladin. His early life involved captivity after the Battle of Inab yet he eventually secured his release and assumed control over his family lands while navigating complex political alliances among the Crusader states. *Early Life and Captivity* describes how Raymond spent roughly ten years as a prisoner of Nur ad-Din Zengi which shaped his diplomatic approach and deep understanding of Islamic leadership tactics that he would later employ to protect his county. This period of imprisonment allowed him to learn Arabic and appreciate Muslim culture making him one of the most enlightened Frankish nobles of his time who often preferred negotiation over rash military action.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles* outlines his tenure as regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy and could not rule without assistance during many critical years. Raymond acted as a stabilizing force against the aggressive faction led by Raynald of Châtillon and Guy of Lusignan whose reckless warmongering threatened the very existence of the Latin Kingdom. He opposed the marriage between Sibylla and Guy because he feared their combined influence would lead to disaster and his predictions proved accurate when internal divisions weakened Christian defenses significantly. His political maneuvering demonstrated a keen intellect focused on long-term survival rather than short-term glory or religious fervor that blinded other leaders to practical realities.\n\n*The Battle of Hattin and Legacy* details the catastrophic defeat where Raymond commanded the vanguard yet survived the encirclement that destroyed the main Crusader army due to his strategic withdrawal from the battlefield. Historians debate whether his retreat was an act of cowardice or a calculated move to preserve forces for future defense but evidence suggests he attempted to warn others about the dangers of marching across arid terrain without water. The loss at Hattin opened the path for Saladin to capture Jerusalem and most other fortified cities within months while Raymond himself died shortly after the battle possibly from illness or despair over the collapse of the kingdom he tried so hard to save. His life represents a tragic figure who understood the enemy better than his peers yet failed to prevent the ultimate downfall of the Crusader presence in the Holy Land due to factional infighting and stubborn pride among fellow nobles who ignored his wise counsel repeatedly.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -5.9367987e-05}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.00084650575}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -3.5762793e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0008191244}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.046925183}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -2.02656e-06}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " until", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.00022694062}, {"token": " death", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.0728842e-06}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -8.3446537e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -3.0994463e-06}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -4.8875927e-06}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.0007179572}, {"token": " played", "logprob": -0.018403396}, {"token": " a", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " pivotal", "logprob": -0.007982619}, {"token": " role", "logprob": 0.0}, {"to